2. Edit invoke.js to replace the following values:



* Replace <channel_name> with the name of the channel the smart contract was deployed on. You can find your CA name under the "Certificate Authorities" section of your connection profile.
* Replace <smart_contract_name> with the name of the installed smart contract. You can get this value from your network operator.
* Edit the contents of submitTransaction to invoke a function inside your smart contract. The invoke.js file is written to invoke the [FabCar smart contract](https://github.com/hyperledger/fabric-samples/tree/release-1.4/chaincode/fabcar). If you want to run the following file to submit a transaction, install FabCar and instantiate the smart contract on one of your channels.



3. Navigate to invoke.js using a terminal and run node invoke.js. If the command runs successfully, you should see the following output:

Transaction has been submitted

If you navigate to your channel by using the console, you are able to see that another block was added by the transaction.



Your transaction might fail if you did not configure an anchor peer on your channel. Unless you manually updated your connection profile, your application needs to use the [Service Discovery](https://hyperledger-fabric.readthedocs.io/en/release-2.2/discovery-overview.html) feature to learn about the peers it needs to submit the transaction to.

